The data structures used for these techniques could include sparse matrices or hash tables to efficiently store and manipulate the large amounts of data involved. For example, the user-item matrix and item-content matrix could be represented as sparse matrices, where the non-zero elements are stored in a compressed format to save memory. The similarity scores could be stored in a hash table to allow for efficient lookups.

