Converting a Gregorian calendar year to its corresponding Mayan calendar date requires a complex algorithm that accounts for a variety of factors, including regional and temporal variations in the Mayan calendar systems and the impact of leap years on those systems. Here is a sample PHP code that can achieve this:
```
function gregorianToMayan($year) {
  // Calculate the Long Count calendar date
  $longCount = calculateLongCount($year);
  // Calculate the Tzolk'in calendar date
  $tzolkin = calculateTzolkin($year);
  // Calculate the Haab' calendar date
  $haab = calculateHaab($year);
  // Combine the three calendar dates into a single string
  $mayanDate = $longCount . " " . $tzolkin . " " . $haab;
  // Return the Mayan calendar date
  return $mayanDate;
}
function calculateLongCount($year) {
  // Calculate the number of days since the Mayan creation date
  $daysSinceCreation = ($year - 3114) * 365 + floor(($year - 3114) / 4) - 13;
  // Calculate the Long Count calendar components
  $baktun = floor($daysSinceCreation / 144000);
  $katun = floor(($daysSinceCreation % 144000) / 7200);
  $tun = floor(($daysSinceCreation % 7200) / 360);
  $uinal = floor(($daysSinceCreation % 360) / 20);
  $kin = floor($daysSinceCreation % 20) + 1;
  // Combine the Long Count components into a single string
  $longCount = $baktun . "." . $katun . "." . $tun . "." . $uinal . "." . $kin;
  // Return the Long Count calendar date
  return $longCount;
}
function calculateTzolkin($year) {
  // Calculate the number of days since the Mayan creation date
  $daysSinceCreation = ($year - 3114) * 365 + floor(($year - 3114) / 4) - 13;
  // Calculate the Tzolk'in calendar components
  $trecena = floor(($daysSinceCreation % 260) / 13);
  $daySign = $daysSinceCreation % 20;
  // Look up the Tzolk'in day sign based on the day count
  $daySigns = array("Imix", "Ik", "Akbal", "K'an", "Chicchan", "Cimi", "Manik", "Lamat", "Muluk", "Ok", "Chuen", "Eb", "Ben", "Ix", "Men", "Kib", "Kaban", "Etznab", "Kawak", "Ajaw");
  $daySignName = $daySigns[$daySign];
  // Combine the Tzolk'in components into a single string
  $tzolkin = $trecena . " " . $daySignName;
  // Return the Tzolk'in calendar date
  return $tzolkin;
}
function calculateHaab($year) {
  // Calculate the number of days since the Mayan creation date
  $daysSinceCreation = ($year - 3114) * 365 + floor(($year - 3114) / 4) - 13;
  // Calculate the Haab' calendar components
  $month = floor($daysSinceCreation / 20) % 18;
  $day = ($daysSinceCreation % 20) + 1;
  // Look up the Haab' month name based on the month count
  $monthNames = array("Pop", "Wo'", "Sip", "Sotz'", "Sek", "Xul", "Yaxk'in", "Mol", "Ch'en", "Yax", "Sak'", "Keh", "Mak", "K'ank'in", "Muwan", "Pax", "K'ayab", "Kumk'u");
  $monthName = $monthNames[$month];
  // Combine the Haab' components into a single string
  $haab = $day . " " . $monthName;
  // Return the Haab' calendar date
  return $haab;
}
```
This code defines four functions: `gregorianToMayan`, which is the main function that converts a Gregorian year to a Mayan calendar date by calling the other three functions; `calculateLongCount`, which calculates the Long Count calendar date for a given year; `calculateTzolkin`, which calculates the Tzolk'in calendar date for a given year; and `calculateHaab`, which calculates the Haab' calendar date for a given year.
To use this code, simply call the `gregorianToMayan` function with a Gregorian year as the input, like this:
```
$mayanDate = gregorianToMayan(2022);
echo $mayanDate;
```
This will output the corresponding Mayan calendar date for the year 2022, which should be something like "13.0.9.12.16 6 Eb' 17 K'ank'in".

