```
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
class Book {
    private:
        std::string title;
        std::string author;
        std::string summary;
    public:
        void setTitle(std::string t) {
            title = t;
        }
        std::string getTitle() {
            return title;
        }
        void setAuthor(std::string a) {
            author = a;
        }
        std::string getAuthor() {
            return author;
        }
        void setSummary(std::string s) {
            summary = s;
        }
        std::string getSummary() {
            return summary;
        }
        bool isEqual(Book other) {
            return (title == other.title && author == other.author && summary == other.summary);
        }
};
int main() {
    Book catcher;
    catcher.setTitle("The Catcher in the Rye");
    catcher.setAuthor("J.D. Salinger");
    catcher.setSummary("The story of Holden Caulfield, a teenage boy who has been expelled from prep school.");
    Book book1;
    book1.setTitle("To Kill a Mockingbird");
    book1.setAuthor("Harper Lee");
    book1.setSummary("A story of racial injustice and the loss of innocence in a small Southern town.");
    Book book2;
    book2.setTitle("1984");
    book2.setAuthor("George Orwell");
    book2.setSummary("A dystopian novel set in a totalitarian society where individuality is suppressed.");
    if (catcher.isEqual(book1)) {
        std::cout << "The Catcher in the Rye and To Kill a Mockingbird are the same book." << std::endl;
    } else {
        std::cout << "The Catcher in the Rye and To Kill a Mockingbird are different books." << std::endl;
    }
    if (catcher.isEqual(book2)) {
        std::cout << "The Catcher in the Rye and 1984 are the same book." << std::endl;
    } else {
        std::cout << "The Catcher in the Rye and 1984 are different books." << std::endl;
    }
    if (book1.isEqual(book2)) {
        std::cout << "To Kill a Mockingbird and 1984 are the same book." << std::endl;
    } else {
        std::cout << "To Kill a Mockingbird and 1984 are different books." << std::endl;
    }
    return 0;
}
```
In this program, we create a class called "Book" with private member variables for the title, author, and summary, and public member functions for setting and getting the values of those variables, and a function to check if two books have the same title, author, and summary. 
We then create three instances of the "Book" class: "catcher" for "The Catcher in the Rye" by J.D. Salinger, and "book1" and "book2" for two other books with different titles, authors, and summaries.
Finally, we use the "isEqual" function to check if any two books have the same title, author, and summary. We compare "catcher" with "book1" and "book2", and "book1" with "book2". 
If two books have the same title, author, and summary, the "isEqual" function will return true, indicating that they are the same book. If they have different values for any of those variables, the function will return false, indicating that they are different books.
Therefore, we can use the "isEqual" function to check if any two books have the same title, author, and summary.

